on run argv
  set keyPath to item 1 of argv
  set pdfPath to item 2 of argv
  with timeout of 900 seconds
    tell application "Keynote"
      set doc to front document
      set presenter notes of slide 1 of doc to "Title. Joint work with my PhD student Sifei Li. A tool paper: the first software that runs causal and counterfactual reasoning directly on the string-diagrammatic semantics."
      set presenter notes of slide 2 of doc to "Roadmap. I will proceed by examples: first what causal identifiability is, on the smoking scenario; then the string diagram surgery view of it; then the questions the tool should answer and the tool itself; finally how it fares against existing implementations."
      set presenter notes of slide 3 of doc to "Part I: what identifiability is, on one example."
      set presenter notes of slide 4 of doc to "Classic example. We observe smokers and non-smokers and whether they develop cancer. Numbers here are from the joint table shipped with the tool's smoking example."
      set presenter notes of slide 5 of doc to "Classic example. We observe smokers and non-smokers and whether they develop cancer. Numbers here are from the joint table shipped with the tool's smoking example. Smokers get cancer far more often: the scientist concludes that smoking causes cancer."
      set presenter notes of slide 6 of doc to "The tobacco company objects: the same data are compatible with a model where a hidden factor, say a genetic trait, causes both the craving and the disease. The two causal models predict the same P(S,C): the data alone cannot separate them. This is the whole difficulty."
      set presenter notes of slide 7 of doc to "Three kinds of question, in increasing order of difficulty (Pearl's ladder). Association is answered by the data. Intervention asks what happens if we force people to smoke: a randomised trial would settle it, but no ethics committee will allow it. Counterfactuals are about a specific individual and an alternative history that never happened: nothing can be observed directly."
      set presenter notes of slide 8 of doc to "Three kinds of question, in increasing order of difficulty (Pearl's ladder). Association is answered by the data. Intervention asks what happens if we force people to smoke: a randomised trial would settle it, but no ethics committee will allow it. Counterfactuals are about a specific individual and an alternative history that never happened: nothing can be observed directly."
      set presenter notes of slide 9 of doc to "Three kinds of question, in increasing order of difficulty (Pearl's ladder). Association is answered by the data. Intervention asks what happens if we force people to smoke: a randomised trial would settle it, but no ethics committee will allow it. Counterfactuals are about a specific individual and an alternative history that never happened: nothing can be observed directly."
      set presenter notes of slide 10 of doc to "Identifiability: given the causal graph and the observational distribution, is the query determined? With only S and C and a hidden common cause (the bow graph) it is not: two models agree on P(S,C) and disagree on the effect. Adding the mediator tar changes everything: the effect is identifiable by the front-door formula. On our data the effect is real but smaller than the association suggests. Complete algorithms exist: ID for interventions, ID* and IDC* for counterfactuals, implemented in cfid and y0."
      set presenter notes of slide 11 of doc to "Identifiability: given the causal graph and the observational distribution, is the query determined? With only S and C and a hidden common cause (the bow graph) it is not: two models agree on P(S,C) and disagree on the effect. Adding the mediator tar changes everything: the effect is identifiable by the front-door formula. On our data the effect is real but smaller than the association suggests. Complete algorithms exist: ID for interventions, ID* and IDC* for counterfactuals, implemented in cfid and y0."
      set presenter notes of slide 12 of doc to "Identifiability: given the causal graph and the observational distribution, is the query determined? With only S and C and a hidden common cause (the bow graph) it is not: two models agree on P(S,C) and disagree on the effect. Adding the mediator tar changes everything: the effect is identifiable by the front-door formula. On our data the effect is real but smaller than the association suggests. Complete algorithms exist: ID for interventions, ID* and IDC* for counterfactuals, implemented in cfid and y0."
      set presenter notes of slide 13 of doc to "Part II: the categorical, diagrammatic view of the same story."
      set presenter notes of slide 14 of doc to "The smoking model as a string diagram, read bottom to top. Wires are variables, boxes are mechanisms, triangles are states, black dots copy. The hidden factor H has no output wire. The whole diagram denotes the joint distribution. The point is that this is an algebraic object, a morphism in a Markov category, not just a picture of a graph: we can compute with it equationally."
      set presenter notes of slide 15 of doc to "The smoking model as a string diagram, read bottom to top. Wires are variables, boxes are mechanisms, triangles are states, black dots copy. The hidden factor H has no output wire. The whole diagram denotes the joint distribution. The point is that this is an algebraic object, a morphism in a Markov category, not just a picture of a graph: we can compute with it equationally."
      set presenter notes of slide 16 of doc to "Intervention is surgery: cut out the box that produces S and plug in the constant state. What remains denotes the interventional distribution. Pearl's do-operator is a local rewrite of the diagram, not a global symbolic substitution. This is the 2019 FoSSaCS paper with Jacobs and Kissinger."
      set presenter notes of slide 17 of doc to "Intervention is surgery: cut out the box that produces S and plug in the constant state. What remains denotes the interventional distribution. Pearl's do-operator is a local rewrite of the diagram, not a global symbolic substitution. This is the 2019 FoSSaCS paper with Jacobs and Kissinger."
      set presenter notes of slide 18 of doc to "Intervention is surgery: cut out the box that produces S and plug in the constant state. What remains denotes the interventional distribution. Pearl's do-operator is a local rewrite of the diagram, not a global symbolic substitution. This is the 2019 FoSSaCS paper with Jacobs and Kissinger."
      set presenter notes of slide 19 of doc to "How does one identify an effect in this language? Comb disintegration: every joint state factors as a comb f wrapped around a channel g, both computable from the observed distribution. Doing surgery on the comb and reading the diagram off gives exactly the front-door formula. Identification is a rewriting process from the interventional diagram to one that only mentions observable pieces."
      set presenter notes of slide 20 of doc to "Counterfactuals need one more idea. First, make every mechanism deterministic: push all the randomness into an explicit noise state. Every conditional distribution factors like this. The payoff is that two scenarios can now be made to share exactly the same randomness."
      set presenter notes of slide 21 of doc to "Step 2, the twin network (Balke and Pearl): the factual world and the counterfactual world side by side. On the left Bob smoked and got cancer: the evidence is imposed on the wires. On the right the surgery replaces the smoking mechanism. The two worlds share the hidden factor and the noise, so they concern the same Bob. The query is the output wire C prime, conditioned on the evidence. Identifiability asks whether this diagram's output is determined by the available distributions."
      set presenter notes of slide 22 of doc to "Step 2, the twin network (Balke and Pearl): the factual world and the counterfactual world side by side. On the left Bob smoked and got cancer: the evidence is imposed on the wires. On the right the surgery replaces the smoking mechanism. The two worlds share the hidden factor and the noise, so they concern the same Bob. The query is the output wire C prime, conditioned on the evidence. Identifiability asks whether this diagram's output is determined by the available distributions."
      set presenter notes of slide 23 of doc to "Why bother? Everything lives in one language: the model, the intervention, the counterfactual and the final estimand are all string diagrams, and every step is an equational rewrite, sound by the laws of the category. That makes the reasoning compositional and, in principle, mechanisable and verifiable. The theory has been developed over several years, but no software implemented it. That is the gap we address."
      set presenter notes of slide 24 of doc to "Part III: the questions behind the tool, and the tool itself."
      set presenter notes of slide 25 of doc to "Three questions. Feasibility: can the semantics be turned into a working tool at all? Challenges: what does it take, concretely? Comparison: how does it fare against the established tools, which are combinatorial graph algorithms with symbolic bookkeeping, on correctness, runtime and transparency?"
      set presenter notes of slide 26 of doc to "The tool: a Julia prototype built on Catlab, whose wiring diagrams are the concrete data structure for string diagrams. Five stages, mirroring the theory one to one: compile the ADMG, build the parallel worlds, simplify, identify, output. Every intermediate diagram is exported to Chyp so one can look at it. Plus a scoped extension computing effects numerically by comb disintegration. Publicly available."
      set presenter notes of slide 27 of doc to "The running example of the paper. Five variables: weather, departure time, mode of transport, lateness, work performance. Departure time and performance are confounded by something unobserved, say recurring engineering works. The counterfactual query: she left at t prime under weather w and takes mode m under that weather; how would she have performed had she left at t?"
      set presenter notes of slide 28 of doc to "Stage 1, tool output. The bidirected edge becomes a fresh latent root R feeding both endpoints (rootification), then each variable gets a mechanism box and a noise source. These are the actual Chyp exports."
      set presenter notes of slide 29 of doc to "Stage 2: the multiverse. One world per clause of the query, mechanisms duplicated, the six exogenous sources shared at the bottom. Surgery and evidence are placed in their own worlds. This is the twin network of Part II, built automatically."
      set presenter notes of slide 30 of doc to "Stage 3: four rewrite passes bring the diagram to a canonical form that splits into R-fragments, the diagrammatic counterpart of c-components. Each pass strictly decreases a structural measure, so it terminates; we fix a topological order, as in Lorenz and Tull's simplify-cf."
      set presenter notes of slide 31 of doc to "Stages 4 and 5. Each R-fragment is rewritten by case analysis into an interventional probability box; composition along a shared wire is summation. The output is the estimand as a formula. If an exogenous source cannot be absorbed, the query is not identifiable, and the tool says where it got stuck."
      set presenter notes of slide 32 of doc to "Closing the loop with the smoking scenario. The comb extension takes the diagram, the observational table and the comb structure, finds the g and f regions on the diagram, checks the decomposition, and computes the effect from the table. Smoking has an effect, but smaller than the association: the confounding inflates it."
      set presenter notes of slide 33 of doc to "Part IV: does it work, and how does it compare?"
      set presenter notes of slide 34 of doc to "Correctness, by differential testing against cfid, the R implementation of ID-CF. 1860 random cases, 98.5 percent agreement. All 28 disagreements were inspected by hand with ID* and IDC*: in 27 our verdict was right; the one miss is a conservative false negative, a missing IDC* reduction. No false positives. cfid is a comparison implementation, not an oracle."
      set presenter notes of slide 35 of doc to "Runtime, warm medians. Against cfid we are consistently faster: 2.7 times on the small drug and party queries, 86 times on the 70-node hepar2 network. y0 is faster on the small queries, a direct graph-level implementation has lower overhead, but 22 times slower on hepar2: the explicit simplification pays off as the model grows."
      set presenter notes of slide 36 of doc to "Synthetic families of controllable size at 32 nodes. Faster than both baselines on chains, fan-in and dense graphs; the one reversal is chain_bi, where every node is confounded: there the simplification stage blows up."
      set presenter notes of slide 37 of doc to "Sweeping the number of nodes: on chain, fanin and dense the advantage grows with n, on chain_bi it stays below one and worsens. The behaviour is structure-dependent, and the culprit is the same: confounding inflates simplification."
      set presenter notes of slide 38 of doc to "The stage breakdown explains it: around 90 percent of the time is simplification; identification is light. cfid does its main work in one call. So the categorical method trades a heavy simplification phase for a light identification phase: a win when simplification shrinks the problem, a loss under dense confounding. Incremental rewriting and better diagram data structures are the obvious fix."
      set presenter notes of slide 39 of doc to "The comb extension against pyAgrum's causalImpact on HEPAR2-derived queries, same tables, identical results. Slower only at the smallest comb; the speedup grows with the comb size."
      set presenter notes of slide 40 of doc to "To conclude. The categorical semantics can be run: the full ID-CF pipeline as rewrites on wiring diagrams, every stage inspectable. It is competitive: high agreement with cfid, no false positives, faster on sparse and moderately dense models. Limitations: scope, the simplification bottleneck under dense confounding, visualisation, and no formal verification yet. All of these are the next steps."
      set presenter notes of slide 41 of doc to "References."
      save doc in (POSIX file keyPath)
      export doc to (POSIX file pdfPath) as PDF with properties {PDF image quality:Good, skipped slides:false, all stages:false}
    end tell
  end timeout
end run